    historyContent: {
      origins: 'Games of this type first appeared in the mid-19th century with special cards. In Britain, Spade the Gardener (families of five) was superseded by Happy Families (John Jaques Jr., before 1851), with four-card families. In the USA, Dr Busby (1843) and Authors (1861) followed. Quartett is the German/Austrian equivalent, also called Ablegspiel in Upper Austria and Styria.',
      originCountries: [
        'United Kingdom',
        'Germany',
        'Austria',
      ],
      timeline: [
        'Before 1851: Happy Families invented by John Jaques Jr.; first published before the Great Exhibition.',
        '1843: Dr Busby (USA), family-based, published.',
        '1861: Authors (USA) published with educational author cards.',
        'Late 19th century: German Quartett packs proliferate; themes range from families to tractors, animals, etc.',
        '1880sâ€“present: Thousands of Quartett designs; Spielzeit catalogue (2000) listed 4800 German Quartett and Schwarzer Peter packs.',
        'Present: Quartett often played with themed decks; can double as Top Trumps (Supertrumpf) when cards have statistics.',
      ],
      evolution: 'British Happy Families used occupational families (Mr/Mrs/Master/Miss). German Quartett expanded to diverse themes (animals, vehicles, etc.). Can be played with a standard 32-card German pack (A-K-D-B-10-9-8-7) or dedicated Quartett packs (typically 8 quartets = 32 cards).',
      cultural: "Classic children's and family game. German Quartett cards often include technical statistics for Top Trumps. Popular in Germany and Austria; Happy Families remains a British staple.",
    },
